# core.autocrlf=true rewrites line endings in anything git decides is text.
# Chunker parts are raw slices of a model or an .exe: if one ever got treated as
# text, git would rewrite CRLF inside it and the part would fail its SHA-256 on
# the next clone -- a corruption that only shows up on someone else's machine.
# Git's heuristic (does it contain a NUL byte?) happens to classify every current
# part as binary, but that is luck, not a guarantee. Pin it.
*.bin binary
manifest.json text eol=lf

# Source stays plain text with LF, so hashes and diffs are stable across OSes.
*.py text eol=lf
*.spec text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf

# Test images are binary.
*.jpg binary
*.png binary
